@preamble use("test")
equal
Asserts that actual and expected are deeply equal. Numbers are compared
by value, strings and symbols by content, lists element-by-element
including shape. Functions and other opaque types compare by identity.

equal(actual, expected)

equal(1 + 1, 2)    # true
===
not_equal
Asserts that actual does not equal expected.

not_equal(actual, expected)

not_equal(1, 2)    # true
===
is_true
Asserts that val is truthy.

is_true(val)

is_true(1 > 0)    # true
===
is_false
Asserts that val is falsy.

is_false(val)

is_false(1 > 2)    # true
===
error
Asserts that val is a shaped error (a list whose first element is @error).

error(val)

error([@error, :test, "oops"])    # true
===
not_error
Asserts that val is not a shaped error.

not_error(val)

not_error(42)    # true
===
run
@unchecked
Runs a named test case. Faults inside fn are caught and reported as
failures without stopping other tests.

run(name, fn)

run("addition", () { equal(1 + 1, 2) })
